WEBVTT

1
00:00:52.702 --> 00:00:55.535
<i>You know that feeling</i>
<i>where no matter what you do</i>

2
00:00:55.572 --> 00:00:58.769
<i>or where you go</i>
<i>you just don't fit in?</i>

3
00:00:58.809 --> 00:01:00.640
<i>I don't know the word for that.</i>

4
00:01:00.677 --> 00:01:03.805
<i>Alienation, estrangement...</i>

5
00:01:03.847 --> 00:01:07.749
<i>incompatibility?</i>
<i>Naw, those ain't right.</i>

6
00:01:07.784 --> 00:01:12.483
<i>But there's gotta be a word for it,</i>
<i>because that's how I feel all the time.</i>

7
00:01:12.522 --> 00:01:15.980
<i>My name's Akeelah Anderson</i>
<i>and I'm 11 years old.</i>

8
00:01:16.026 --> 00:01:19.257
<i>And this all starts</i>
<i>at Crenshaw Middle School</i>

9
00:01:19.296 --> 00:01:21.594
<i>in South Los Angeles.</i>

10
00:01:35.312 --> 00:01:39.544
You're all in the seventh grade now
and I know you can do better than this.

11
00:01:39.583 --> 00:01:43.417
So when I give you
a list of words, study them.

12
00:01:43.453 --> 00:01:45.318
Akeelah!

13
00:01:45.355 --> 00:01:47.619
How long did you study
for this spelling test?

14
00:01:50.527 --> 00:01:52.085
I didn't.

15
00:01:55.298 --> 00:01:59.098
See me after class.
Okay, you two need to turn around.

16
00:01:59.136 --> 00:02:01.331
Enough!

17
00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:06.200
You know, you could be
one of my very best students.

18
00:02:06.243 --> 00:02:08.370
But you don't turn in
half your homework.

19
00:02:08.411 --> 00:02:10.709
Sometimes you don't even
show up for class.

20
00:02:10.747 --> 00:02:13.147
- What's going on?
- I don't know.

21
00:02:18.021 --> 00:02:19.386
Have you heard of...

22
00:02:21.191 --> 00:02:23.318
have you heard
of next week's spelling bee?

23
00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:25.294
No.

24
00:02:25.328 --> 00:02:27.319
Well, I think you should
sign up for it.

25
00:02:32.435 --> 00:02:34.767
Does he really think I'm
gonna be walking around

26
00:02:34.804 --> 00:02:37.739
with somebody in little shorts
like that? He crazy!

27
00:02:37.774 --> 00:02:41.301
- Ooh, is Devon coming back today?
- Yeah. Got a two-week leave.

28
00:02:41.344 --> 00:02:45.178
Your brother is fine!
I got it all figured out... look.

29
00:02:45.215 --> 00:02:47.809
He gonna be the pilot
on a big commercial jet

30
00:02:47.851 --> 00:02:50.342
and I'm gonna be
the flight attendant.

31
00:02:50.387 --> 00:02:52.947
Got any change
for an old man, girls?

32
00:02:52.989 --> 00:02:54.889
Ain't got no change for myself.

33
00:02:58.361 --> 00:03:01.023
Damn, Derrick-T's new ride is tight!

34
00:03:02.933 --> 00:03:05.697
He's been trying to get
Terrence in trouble.

35
00:03:05.735 --> 00:03:08.226
Girl, your bro can get
his own self in trouble.

36
00:03:08.271 --> 00:03:10.933
- So what'd Ms. Cross want?
- Nothin'.

37
00:03:10.974 --> 00:03:13.499
Just talking about
some stupid-ass spelling bee.

38
00:03:13.543 --> 00:03:15.511
You gonna do it?

39
00:03:15.545 --> 00:03:18.343
- You'd probably be good.
- And get up in front of everybody?

40
00:03:18.381 --> 00:03:20.611
I'd probably pee in my pants.

41
00:03:21.618 --> 00:03:24.451
Tell me about the boy in your class
with the itty-bitty shorts.

42
00:03:24.487 --> 00:03:25.545
I think he's cute.

43
00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:31.491
<i>Yes!</i>

44
00:03:40.337 --> 00:03:42.168
Yes!

45
00:03:42.205 --> 00:03:45.140
- Keelie, Mama says go eat.
- I'm about to get a high score.

46
00:03:45.175 --> 00:03:46.608
- One minute.
- I don't care.

47
00:03:46.643 --> 00:03:48.110
Mama says go eat!

48
00:03:48.144 --> 00:03:51.341
Oh yeah! I've been dreaming about
your cooking for the past five months.

49
00:03:51.381 --> 00:03:52.746
Let me take that.

50
00:03:52.782 --> 00:03:55.342
At least I got one baby
appreciate what I do around here.

51
00:03:55.385 --> 00:03:58.548
We all appreciate you.
Hey... give me some sugar.

52
00:03:58.588 --> 00:04:01.250
- Where is Terrence?
- I don't know. Maybe still at practice.

53
00:04:01.291 --> 00:04:03.452
Practice was over an hour ago.
I know one thing...

54
00:04:03.493 --> 00:04:05.586
he better not be hanging out
with no Derrick-T.

55
00:04:05.629 --> 00:04:07.597
Derrick-T?
That fool still alive?

56
00:04:07.631 --> 00:04:09.963
- Not after you get done with him.
- That's right.

57
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:11.831
- Hey, you.
- So...

58
00:04:11.868 --> 00:04:13.893
how many planes have you
shot down so far?

59
00:04:13.937 --> 00:04:16.565
So far zero. But you don't do
too much shooting sitting behind

60
00:04:16.606 --> 00:04:18.699
- a computer screen in Nevada.
- Oooh, good.

61
00:04:18.742 --> 00:04:21.040
You stay on the ground.
Let them white boys go up there.

62
00:04:21.077 --> 00:04:24.069
- You stay down here where you belong.
- Mama, I'm gonna have my wings

63
00:04:24.114 --> 00:04:27.311
and my college degree before you know
it... unless this one beats me to it.

64
00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:29.580
Not skipping class
with Georgia Cavanaugh, she won't.

65
00:04:29.619 --> 00:04:32.179
- Skipping class?
- Only PE.

66
00:04:32.222 --> 00:04:35.658
- You better not be skipping no class.
- Akeelah, go turn off that television.

67
00:04:35.692 --> 00:04:37.990
Hey, hey... flip it over
to ESPN real quick

68
00:04:38.028 --> 00:04:40.360
and let me check out
that Laker score, all right?

69
00:04:40.397 --> 00:04:42.388
<i>I need you to talk to Terrence...</i>

70
00:04:42.432 --> 00:04:44.866
<i>... no more poisonous</i>
<i>sprays, no more glue traps,</i>

71
00:04:44.901 --> 00:04:48.132
and no more throwing your money away.
The scientific design of the...

72
00:04:48.171 --> 00:04:51.834
If you spell the next word correctly,
you will be the champion.

73
00:04:51.875 --> 00:04:55.106
- What's this?
- Akeelah, turn off the TV now.

74
00:04:55.145 --> 00:04:57.841
<i>- "Brunneous."</i>
- B...

75
00:04:57.881 --> 00:05:01.078
R-U-N...

76
00:05:01.117 --> 00:05:04.484
N-E-O-U-S.

77
00:05:04.521 --> 00:05:06.580
- Brunneous.
<i>- Congratulations.</i>

78
00:05:06.623 --> 00:05:09.922
<i>You are the Scripps</i>
<i>National Spelling Bee Champion.</i>

79
00:05:11.961 --> 00:05:13.986
Akeelah needs to listen!

80
00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:16.021
Turn it off now!

81
00:05:18.568 --> 00:05:20.160
Brunneous...

82
00:05:20.203 --> 00:05:22.694
brunneous?

83
00:05:26.209 --> 00:05:28.905
"Brunneous: dark brown;

84
00:05:28.945 --> 00:05:31.641
used chiefly scientifically."

85
00:05:31.681 --> 00:05:34.241
Well, why can't they just say brown?

86
00:05:36.453 --> 00:05:39.286
Daddy, have you ever
heard of this word?

87
00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:47.257
Yeah, probably did.

88
00:05:52.302 --> 00:05:55.237
Cauterize, C-A-U-T-E-R-I-Z-E.

89
00:05:55.271 --> 00:05:59.537
Fortification,
F-O-R-T-I-F-I-C-A-T-I-O-N.

90
00:05:59.576 --> 00:06:03.774
Correspond,
C-O-R-R-E-S-P-O-N-D...

91
00:06:15.091 --> 00:06:18.458
<i>... construction on our northeast lot,</i>

92
00:06:18.495 --> 00:06:21.328
<i>traffic will be directed</i>
<i>to the side alley.</i>

93
00:06:21.364 --> 00:06:25.425
<i>This will be through the month.</i>
<i>Drop-offs will be from the south gate.</i>

94
00:06:27.337 --> 00:06:29.066
Hey, freak.

95
00:06:30.073 --> 00:06:32.337
We want you to take care
of our English homework.

96
00:06:32.375 --> 00:06:35.276
- Everybody say you a brainiac.
- I ain't no brainiac.

97
00:06:35.311 --> 00:06:39.714
Like hell you ain't!
Always got them A's down, right?

98
00:06:39.749 --> 00:06:41.478
Yeah, she's scared of us.

99
00:06:41.518 --> 00:06:42.712
Don't run away...

100
00:06:42.752 --> 00:06:45.721
I cannot thank you enough
for coming down here today, Josh.

101
00:06:45.755 --> 00:06:48.087
I've got the district
breathing down my back.

102
00:06:48.124 --> 00:06:50.558
The test scores have
gone down again this year.

103
00:06:50.593 --> 00:06:53.562
As I said earlier,
I appreciate your dilemma, Bob,

104
00:06:53.596 --> 00:06:57.327
but I really don't understand
what it is I have to offer.

105
00:06:57.367 --> 00:06:59.858
No no no, all I want you to do
is look at these kids

106
00:06:59.903 --> 00:07:02.235
and tell me if you think
my idea's crazy.

107
00:07:02.272 --> 00:07:04.467
I think some of them
have a lot of potential.

108
00:07:04.507 --> 00:07:06.236
Girls!
Why aren't you in class?

109
00:07:06.276 --> 00:07:08.744
- She holding us up.
- Akeelah!

110
00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:13.174
Are you signed up
for the spelling bee today?

111
00:07:13.216 --> 00:07:14.581
No.

112
00:07:14.617 --> 00:07:17.586
I want you to come
to my office, please.

113
00:07:20.156 --> 00:07:23.023
So, Ms. Cross tells me

114
00:07:23.059 --> 00:07:25.687
you've never missed a word
on your spelling tests.

115
00:07:25.728 --> 00:07:28.822
But your attendance record
leaves a little to be desired.

116
00:07:29.833 --> 00:07:33.428
- You're 11, did you skip a grade?
- The second.

117
00:07:35.472 --> 00:07:39.841
Akeelah, have you ever heard
of the Scripps National Spelling Bee?

118
00:07:39.876 --> 00:07:43.243
- I think it was on TV last week.
- Yes!

119
00:07:43.279 --> 00:07:45.804
Yes, they show it
on ESPN every year.

120
00:07:45.849 --> 00:07:48.613
Middle school students
from all over the country

121
00:07:48.651 --> 00:07:52.712
compete in school district
and regional spelling bees

122
00:07:52.755 --> 00:07:56.521
trying to make it to the national
spelling bee in Washington DC.

123
00:07:56.559 --> 00:07:59.323
Next year, I want
one of our students there.

124
00:07:59.362 --> 00:08:02.160
So whoever wins
the school bee today

125
00:08:02.198 --> 00:08:05.429
gets to represent Crenshaw
at the district bee

126
00:08:05.468 --> 00:08:07.800
- next month.
- Why would anybody want

127
00:08:07.837 --> 00:08:11.364
to represents a school that can't even
put doors on the toilet stalls?

128
00:08:14.644 --> 00:08:17.306
Akeelah, if we can't show
our students can perform,

129
00:08:17.347 --> 00:08:20.373
we're not going to have money for books,
let alone bathroom doors.

130
00:08:20.416 --> 00:08:22.543
Now, I want you to do
the bee today, all right?

131
00:08:22.585 --> 00:08:25.247
So everybody can call me
a freak and a brainiac?

132
00:08:25.288 --> 00:08:28.189
No, I ain't down
for no spelling bee.

133
00:08:29.225 --> 00:08:33.161
Well, maybe you'd be "down" for spending
the rest of the semester

134
00:08:33.196 --> 00:08:36.097
in detention
for all of your absences.

135
00:08:36.132 --> 00:08:38.123
I'd like to welcome you all

136
00:08:38.167 --> 00:08:41.364
to Crenshaw's first schoolwide
spelling bee.

137
00:08:41.404 --> 00:08:45.101
We have some very special students
competing today,

138
00:08:45.141 --> 00:08:47.336
so let's give them all
a round of applause.

139
00:08:50.113 --> 00:08:52.104
Whoo!

140
00:08:53.416 --> 00:08:56.442
Okay, we drew numbers to see
who would go first,

141
00:08:56.486 --> 00:08:59.922
and that's Chuckie Johnson
from the eighth grade.

142
00:09:03.226 --> 00:09:05.626
Mr. Welch, I have a question
about the basketball nets.

143
00:09:05.662 --> 00:09:08.756
Chuckie!
You need to pay attention.

144
00:09:08.798 --> 00:09:12.564
Now, we're gonna start things off
with "grovel."

145
00:09:12.602 --> 00:09:14.627
Gravel, like little rocks?

146
00:09:15.838 --> 00:09:18.102
No, grovel like

147
00:09:18.141 --> 00:09:20.473
get down
on your knees and grovel.

148
00:09:20.510 --> 00:09:22.876
Get down on my knees?
What?!

149
00:09:22.912 --> 00:09:24.812
<i>Just spell the word.</i>

150
00:09:24.847 --> 00:09:27.645
Uh, G-R...

151
00:09:27.684 --> 00:09:30.482
A-V-E-L.

152
00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:32.852
No, Chuckie, you spelled gravel.

153
00:09:32.889 --> 00:09:36.655
<i>I meant grovel: G-R-O-V-E-L.</i>

154
00:09:36.693 --> 00:09:39.389
You weren't listening.
I'm sorry, Chuckie.

155
00:09:39.429 --> 00:09:42.057
- Who cares?
- Okay, next up,

156
00:09:42.098 --> 00:09:44.828
<i>Akeelah Anderson.</i>

157
00:09:44.867 --> 00:09:47.461
Oh, you go, girl!

158
00:09:48.538 --> 00:09:52.133
Akeelah, your word is "doubt."

159
00:09:52.175 --> 00:09:55.633
- Doubt.
- D-O-U-B-T.

160
00:09:55.678 --> 00:09:58.169
I'm sorry, Akeelah,
you have to speak up.

161
00:09:58.214 --> 00:10:00.341
D-O-U-B-T.

162
00:10:00.383 --> 00:10:02.146
Very good.

163
00:10:05.321 --> 00:10:07.482
<i>Next, Calvin Baker...</i>

164
00:10:07.523 --> 00:10:10.651
P-L-A-S-I-D.

165
00:10:10.693 --> 00:10:12.786
Placid.

166
00:10:12.829 --> 00:10:16.595
I'm sorry,
it's P-L-A-C-I-D.

167
00:10:20.503 --> 00:10:23.063
Okay, Akeelah,
if you get this word

168
00:10:23.106 --> 00:10:25.768
you will be the winner.
"Fanciful."

169
00:10:25.808 --> 00:10:28.368
- Fan...
- F-A-N-C-I-F-U-L.

170
00:10:28.411 --> 00:10:30.345
- <i>Whoa.</i>
- That is correct,

171
00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:33.440
and you have won Crenshaw's
inaugural spelling bee.

172
00:10:33.483 --> 00:10:35.110
Whoo!

173
00:10:35.151 --> 00:10:36.482
Whatever.

174
00:10:41.024 --> 00:10:42.855
Excuse me.

175
00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:46.119
Spell "prestidigitation."

176
00:10:47.697 --> 00:10:50.860
I'm sorry, sir,
but this girl is only 11,

177
00:10:50.900 --> 00:10:54.165
<i>and she's already won.</i>
<i>Mr. Welch, what is this?</i>

178
00:10:56.239 --> 00:10:59.902
Prestidigitation,
can you spell it?

179
00:11:08.051 --> 00:11:13.683
P-R-E-S-T-I-D-I-G-I-T-A-T-I-O-N,

180
00:11:13.723 --> 00:11:16.521
prestidigitation.

181
00:11:16.559 --> 00:11:18.618
That's correct.

182
00:11:18.661 --> 00:11:20.652
Whoo!

183
00:11:22.732 --> 00:11:28.034
- "Ambidextrous."
- A-M-B-I-D-E-X-T-R-O-U-S.

184
00:11:28.071 --> 00:11:32.701
- "Pterodactyl."
- P-T-E-R-O-D-A-C-T-Y-L.

185
00:11:32.742 --> 00:11:36.303
- "Pulchritude."
- P...

186
00:11:39.916 --> 00:11:42.510
P-U-L-C...

187
00:11:45.788 --> 00:11:47.483
R...

188
00:11:47.523 --> 00:11:50.617
I-T-U-D-E...

189
00:11:51.828 --> 00:11:54.388
pulchritude?

190
00:11:54.430 --> 00:11:58.366
That's incorrect.
It's from the Latin root "pulcher"

191
00:11:58.401 --> 00:12:01.837
and there's an H after the C.

192
00:12:04.607 --> 00:12:06.871
<i>See?</i>
<i>She ain't so smart.</i>

193
00:12:13.416 --> 00:12:16.078
- Run for it! See ya!
- Bye, sweetie.

194
00:12:17.587 --> 00:12:19.714
Akeelah, wait!

195
00:12:19.756 --> 00:12:21.747
Where are you going?
You did great.

196
00:12:21.791 --> 00:12:24.191
You were spelling words
I don't know how to spell.

197
00:12:24.227 --> 00:12:28.129
Mr. Welch, I told you I did not want
to do this. They're laughing at me.

198
00:12:28.164 --> 00:12:30.894
They laugh
because you intimidate them.

199
00:12:30.933 --> 00:12:34.460
But if you'd stood your ground,
you might have earned their respect.

200
00:12:34.504 --> 00:12:38.167
Bob, the girl has potential
but she needs to be coached.

201
00:12:40.710 --> 00:12:42.575
Do you know who that was?

202
00:12:42.612 --> 00:12:44.807
That was Dr. Joshua Larabee.

203
00:12:44.847 --> 00:12:47.145
<i>He used to chair</i>
<i>the English department at UCLA.</i>

204
00:12:47.183 --> 00:12:49.811
He and I went to college together.
But get this...

205
00:12:49.852 --> 00:12:53.015
when he was a kid, he went all the way
to the national spelling bee.

206
00:12:53.055 --> 00:12:55.990
And he thinks you have a chance
of going there yourself.

207
00:12:56.025 --> 00:12:58.016
I'm sorry,

208
00:12:58.060 --> 00:12:59.960
but I ain't doin'
no more spelling bees.

209
00:12:59.996 --> 00:13:02.021
Akeelah...

210
00:13:03.633 --> 00:13:06.625
Girl, you kicked some major booty
on that stage today.

211
00:13:06.669 --> 00:13:09.297
But I couldn't spell pulchritude.

212
00:13:09.338 --> 00:13:12.136
But you knocked all them other words
right back at that dude.

213
00:13:12.175 --> 00:13:16.077
They were just trick words. Everybody
knows pterodactyl starts with a P.

214
00:13:16.112 --> 00:13:20.139
Girl, if I could spell like you,
I know I could be a flight attendant.

215
00:13:23.019 --> 00:13:25.112
<i>Keelie, hey, wake up.</i>

216
00:13:27.256 --> 00:13:29.053
Hey.

217
00:13:30.159 --> 00:13:33.617
- Dev, are you leaving?
- Yeah, gotta get back to the base.

218
00:13:33.663 --> 00:13:36.461
Hey, your principal
called Mama.

219
00:13:36.499 --> 00:13:39.263
Said you did real good
in the spelling bee last week.

220
00:13:39.302 --> 00:13:42.169
- I messed up a word.
<i>- He said you got a lot of them right.</i>

221
00:13:42.205 --> 00:13:44.230
He also said
you've got an opportunity

222
00:13:44.273 --> 00:13:46.332
to go to an even bigger
contest next week.

223
00:13:46.375 --> 00:13:49.003
- I don't want to do it.
- Why not?

224
00:13:49.045 --> 00:13:51.104
Everybody's gonna be
looking at me

225
00:13:51.147 --> 00:13:53.411
and there's gonna be
tons of words I don't know.

226
00:13:53.449 --> 00:13:55.883
So you're scared, huh?

227
00:13:55.918 --> 00:13:59.046
<i>How do you think I felt the first time</i>
<i>I had to jump out of an airplane?</i>

228
00:13:59.088 --> 00:14:01.716
I mean, my whole body
said "Don't do it."

229
00:14:01.757 --> 00:14:05.352
But sometimes your brain gotta be
smarter than your body, you know?

230
00:14:05.394 --> 00:14:07.294
But I don't like my school.

231
00:14:07.330 --> 00:14:09.992
I don't see why I gotta do
anything for them.

232
00:14:12.468 --> 00:14:15.028
Then do it for Dad.

233
00:14:15.071 --> 00:14:17.096
I mean, you know
how he was about words.

234
00:14:17.139 --> 00:14:19.664
He'd have loved to see you
do something like this.

235
00:14:19.709 --> 00:14:22.974
- What'd Mama say about it?
- You know how Mama is.

236
00:14:23.012 --> 00:14:25.344
She got a million things
to worry about.

237
00:14:25.381 --> 00:14:27.576
<i>Tell you what,</i>

238
00:14:27.617 --> 00:14:29.551
just do this contest,

239
00:14:29.585 --> 00:14:31.746
and if you make it all the way to DC,

240
00:14:31.787 --> 00:14:33.948
I will parachute down to see you.

241
00:14:33.990 --> 00:14:35.753
<i>Would you like that?</i>

242
00:14:52.975 --> 00:14:56.741
That is a wonderful decision, Akeelah,
a wonderful decision.

243
00:14:56.779 --> 00:14:59.714
You think maybe the school
could buy me a new outfit,

244
00:14:59.749 --> 00:15:02.149
you know, for the district bee?

245
00:15:02.184 --> 00:15:05.176
Uh... I don't know, maybe.

246
00:15:05.221 --> 00:15:09.282
If you make it to the state regional,
maybe something could be arranged.

247
00:15:09.325 --> 00:15:13.193
But you're gonna have to finish in
the top 10 at the district level first.

248
00:15:13.229 --> 00:15:16.687
And, Akeelah, you're going to be going
up against kids from Santa Monica,

249
00:15:16.732 --> 00:15:18.666
Woodland Hills, Beverly Hills.

250
00:15:18.701 --> 00:15:22.262
Some of these kids have been doing this
for years and never even made it to DC.

251
00:15:22.305 --> 00:15:25.035
- Well, maybe I should just give up now.
- No no no.

252
00:15:25.074 --> 00:15:28.976
No, I'm just saying
you need to train hard.

253
00:15:29.011 --> 00:15:31.809
- With Dr. Larabee.
- What?

254
00:15:31.847 --> 00:15:34.611
I don't need any help from him.
I could do this on my own.

255
00:15:34.650 --> 00:15:37.414
Akeelah, the best spellers
in the world have coaches.

256
00:15:37.453 --> 00:15:39.648
<i>I finally got him to consider</i>
<i>taking some time</i>

257
00:15:39.689 --> 00:15:41.748
during his sabbatical
to work with you.

258
00:15:41.791 --> 00:15:44.351
Please, just talk with him.

259
00:15:44.393 --> 00:15:46.861
Here.
He doesn't live far from here.

260
00:15:46.896 --> 00:15:48.864
He lives in this neighborhood?

261
00:15:48.898 --> 00:15:51.366
I thought you said
he was important.

262
00:15:51.400 --> 00:15:54.164
And take this, it's a copy
of last year's spelling bee.

263
00:15:54.203 --> 00:15:57.297
<i>- "Sacciform."</i>
<i>- May I have the definition, please?</i>

264
00:15:57.340 --> 00:15:58.739
<i>Resembling a pouch.</i>

265
00:15:58.774 --> 00:16:00.765
May I have the language
of origin, please?

266
00:16:00.810 --> 00:16:02.573
<i>- Latin.</i>
- Latin?

267
00:16:02.611 --> 00:16:05.671
I thought this was
supposed to be English.

268
00:16:05.715 --> 00:16:07.808
<i>... I-F-O-R...</i>

269
00:16:09.819 --> 00:16:12.879
"Scutellate"?
Can I have the definition?

270
00:16:14.523 --> 00:16:16.684
"Longicollis."

271
00:16:16.726 --> 00:16:18.990
- "Ginglymus."
- "Empennage."

272
00:16:19.028 --> 00:16:21.019
- "Souchong."
- "Intussusception."

273
00:16:21.063 --> 00:16:23.395
- Hey, what's dat?
<i>- Any alternate pronunciations?</i>

274
00:16:23.432 --> 00:16:26.560
Where you been?
Mama's worried.

275
00:16:26.602 --> 00:16:28.627
Mama should just chill, a'ight?

276
00:16:28.671 --> 00:16:31.799
- What you looking at?
- Spelling bee.

277
00:16:31.841 --> 00:16:34.309
Oh yeah, I heard about that.

278
00:16:34.343 --> 00:16:36.971
You going up against
a bunch of rich white kids.

279
00:16:38.014 --> 00:16:40.005
They gonna tear your black ass up.

280
00:16:41.917 --> 00:16:43.817
<i>Intussusception is</i>

281
00:16:43.853 --> 00:16:49.086
<i>I-N-T-U-S-S-U-S-C-E-P-T-I-O-N.</i>

282
00:17:22.558 --> 00:17:24.526
You're late.

283
00:17:24.560 --> 00:17:27.688
- You didn't answer the door.
- That's because you're late.

284
00:17:27.730 --> 00:17:29.698
Come in.

285
00:17:29.732 --> 00:17:31.893
Come come come.

286
00:17:36.172 --> 00:17:38.504
So...

287
00:17:38.541 --> 00:17:41.374
- you want to learn how to spell.
- I know how to spell.

288
00:17:41.410 --> 00:17:44.402
- Spell "staphylococci."
- Um...

289
00:17:44.447 --> 00:17:47.041
S-T-A-F...

290
00:17:47.083 --> 00:17:51.144
There is no F. It's derived
from the Greek so there can't be an F.

291
00:17:51.187 --> 00:17:54.054
Staphylococci:
S-T-A...

292
00:17:54.090 --> 00:17:56.888
P-H-Y...

293
00:17:56.926 --> 00:17:59.258
L-O-C-O...

294
00:17:59.295 --> 00:18:00.990
C-C-I.

295
00:18:01.030 --> 00:18:02.964
<i>Winning word,</i>
<i>national spelling bee</i>

296
00:18:02.998 --> 00:18:04.625
<i>1987.</i>

297
00:18:04.667 --> 00:18:07.135
The first thing
most serious spellers do

298
00:18:07.169 --> 00:18:10.605
is learn all of the winning words
and their origins.

299
00:18:10.639 --> 00:18:12.766
Well, maybe I ain't that serious.

300
00:18:13.809 --> 00:18:16.107
Maybe neither am I.

301
00:18:16.145 --> 00:18:18.375
So why are you home
during the day?

302
00:18:18.414 --> 00:18:20.575
- Ain't you got a job?
<i>- Do me a favor,</i>

303
00:18:20.616 --> 00:18:22.743
leave the ghetto talk
outside, all right?

304
00:18:22.785 --> 00:18:24.650
Ghetto talk?
I don't talk ghetto.

305
00:18:24.687 --> 00:18:26.484
Hm, "ain't you got no job?"

306
00:18:26.522 --> 00:18:29.491
<i>You use that language</i>
<i>to fit in with your friends.</i>

307
00:18:29.525 --> 00:18:32.323
Here you will speak properly
or you won't speak at all.

308
00:18:32.361 --> 00:18:34.420
<i>- Understood?</i>
- Yeah.

309
00:18:35.431 --> 00:18:37.558
Whatever.

310
00:18:37.600 --> 00:18:40.000
- You can leave now.
- Excuse me?

311
00:18:40.035 --> 00:18:42.094
- I said you can leave.
- How come?

312
00:18:42.138 --> 00:18:46.336
Because I don't have time
to waste on insolent little girls.

313
00:18:46.375 --> 00:18:49.538
Insolent?
I ain't insol...

314
00:18:49.578 --> 00:18:51.307
I mean I'm not insolent.

315
00:18:51.347 --> 00:18:54.180
It's just the first thing you do
is start doggin' on...

316
00:18:55.184 --> 00:18:57.618
criticizing the way I speak.

317
00:18:59.555 --> 00:19:02.149
I thought this was
just about spelling words.

318
00:19:04.960 --> 00:19:07.087
Well then, fine.

319
00:19:13.936 --> 00:19:16.234
You know what?

320
00:19:16.272 --> 00:19:20.333
When I put my mind to it,
I can memorize anything.

321
00:19:20.376 --> 00:19:24.073
And I don't need help
from a dictatorial, truculent,

322
00:19:24.113 --> 00:19:26.843
supercilious gardener.

323
00:19:28.150 --> 00:19:30.516
I'm sorry to be so insolent.

324
00:19:39.528 --> 00:19:43.726
Lymphomatotic: L-Y-M...
Laboratory: L-A-B-O-R...

325
00:19:43.766 --> 00:19:46.098
Preglacial...

326
00:19:46.135 --> 00:19:48.069
Irreferable: I-R-R...

327
00:19:48.103 --> 00:19:50.264
Erythroplasia: E-R-Y-T-H...

328
00:19:50.306 --> 00:19:52.570
- Microzoospore...
<i>- Hey.</i>

329
00:19:52.608 --> 00:19:54.508
Baby, what are you still doing up?

330
00:19:54.543 --> 00:19:56.340
I've gotta learn more words.

331
00:19:56.378 --> 00:19:59.677
You gonna come see me
in the district bee this Saturday?

332
00:19:59.715 --> 00:20:02.741
- What, is that at your school?
- Naw.

333
00:20:02.785 --> 00:20:05.151
- Beverly Hills.
- Beverly Hills?

334
00:20:06.555 --> 00:20:09.023
Look, you got other homework.

335
00:20:09.058 --> 00:20:12.687
You need to focus on that. I don't want
you spending all your time on this game.

336
00:20:12.728 --> 00:20:15.526
It ain't a game!
Are you gonna come see me in it?

337
00:20:16.966 --> 00:20:19.526
You know I work at the hospital
on Saturday.

338
00:20:19.568 --> 00:20:21.468
Maybe Kiana can go with you.

339
00:20:21.503 --> 00:20:24.301
As long as she don't bring
that whiny baby.

340
00:20:27.309 --> 00:20:30.574
Come on, girls, let's go.
We're gonna be late.

341
00:20:41.624 --> 00:20:43.319
Dang.

342
00:20:43.359 --> 00:20:45.827
I hope they ain't all here to spell.

343
00:20:45.861 --> 00:20:47.886
Okay, go get your number.

344
00:20:47.930 --> 00:20:50.763
- We'll be sitting back here.
- Good luck, Keelie.

345
00:21:09.985 --> 00:21:11.509
Hi. Name?

346
00:21:11.553 --> 00:21:14.078
Akeelah Anderson.

347
00:21:14.123 --> 00:21:16.182
Right here at the top.

348
00:21:16.225 --> 00:21:20.628
I think you're the first speller we've
ever had from Crenshaw Middle School.

349
00:21:21.630 --> 00:21:23.928
Um... how many kids
are in this thing?

350
00:21:23.966 --> 00:21:25.957
139.

351
00:21:26.969 --> 00:21:28.994
Next.

352
00:21:33.309 --> 00:21:37.643
Need some help?
Don't worry, I won't impale you.

353
00:21:37.680 --> 00:21:40.240
- This your first time?
- Yeah.

354
00:21:40.282 --> 00:21:42.341
- You?
- Second year.

355
00:21:42.384 --> 00:21:45.945
I made it to nationals last year,
finished 13th.

356
00:21:45.988 --> 00:21:47.512
Lucky 13.

357
00:21:47.556 --> 00:21:49.990
You went all the way to DC?

358
00:21:50.025 --> 00:21:52.858
Three of us made it from my school
in Woodland Hills.

359
00:21:52.895 --> 00:21:55.056
See that kid right there?

360
00:21:55.097 --> 00:21:56.997
That's Dylan Chiu.

361
00:21:57.032 --> 00:22:00.229
He's come in second place
at nationals for two years in a row.

362
00:22:00.269 --> 00:22:03.397
This is his last year and everybody
thinks he's gonna win.

363
00:22:03.439 --> 00:22:06.374
Frankly, I'd like to shove him
off a steep precipice.

364
00:22:08.043 --> 00:22:09.704
Hey, my name is Javier.

365
00:22:09.745 --> 00:22:11.736
- I'm Akeelah.
- Hope you make the top 10.

366
00:22:11.780 --> 00:22:13.771
I hope I'm not the first one out.

367
00:22:13.816 --> 00:22:16.444
<i>Will all the spellers take</i>
<i>their positions on stage, please?</i>

368
00:22:16.485 --> 00:22:19.545
Remember, if you don't know how to spell
a word, spell it the way it sounds.

369
00:22:19.588 --> 00:22:23.183
Kids mess up all the time when they
think they're being thrown curveballs,

370
00:22:23.225 --> 00:22:25.352
<i>- and they're not. Good luck.</i>
- Good luck to you too.

371
00:22:25.394 --> 00:22:28.090
<i>- Thank you.</i>
<i>- E...</i>

372
00:22:28.130 --> 00:22:30.257
<i>M...</i>

373
00:22:30.299 --> 00:22:33.166
<i>B-R...</i>

374
00:22:33.202 --> 00:22:35.295
<i>I...</i>

375
00:22:35.337 --> 00:22:37.805
C-A...

376
00:22:38.807 --> 00:22:42.072
T-I-O-N,

377
00:22:42.111 --> 00:22:43.976
embrocation.

378
00:22:53.288 --> 00:22:56.280
- "Rhesus."
- Recess?

379
00:22:56.325 --> 00:22:58.190
<i>May I have a definition, please?</i>

380
00:22:58.227 --> 00:23:01.890
A brownish yellow monkey of India.

381
00:23:01.930 --> 00:23:04.330
Ah, rhesus.

382
00:23:04.366 --> 00:23:06.527
R-H-E-S-U-S,

383
00:23:06.568 --> 00:23:08.900
rhesus.

384
00:23:08.937 --> 00:23:11.132
That's correct.

385
00:23:24.119 --> 00:23:25.984
Number 54?

386
00:23:35.063 --> 00:23:36.997
<i>"Eminent."</i>

387
00:23:40.769 --> 00:23:42.828
<i>Did you hear the word?</i>

388
00:23:42.871 --> 00:23:47.968
I'm not sure if you're saying
imminent or eminent.

389
00:23:48.010 --> 00:23:51.537
- Would you like a definition?
- That'd be cool.

390
00:23:54.817 --> 00:23:59.254
<i>Eminent: rising above</i>
<i>other things or places.</i>

391
00:23:59.288 --> 00:24:01.449
High; lofty...

392
00:24:01.490 --> 00:24:03.856
<i>eminent.</i>

393
00:24:03.892 --> 00:24:09.489
E-M-I-N-E-N-T,

394
00:24:09.531 --> 00:24:13.160
- eminent?
- That's correct.

395
00:24:24.379 --> 00:24:26.609
<i>That's correct.</i>

396
00:24:32.321 --> 00:24:34.448
"Hypertrophic."

397
00:24:34.490 --> 00:24:37.391
H-Y-P-E-R-T-R-O-P-H-I-C,

398
00:24:37.426 --> 00:24:39.724
- hypertrophic.
<i>- That's correct.</i>

399
00:24:48.337 --> 00:24:50.828
<i>That's correct.</i>

400
00:24:55.677 --> 00:24:57.838
<i>"Concierge."</i>

401
00:24:57.880 --> 00:25:00.246
Is that like a guy who
stands around in a hotel?

402
00:25:00.282 --> 00:25:02.273
<i>Speak into the mike, please.</i>

403
00:25:02.317 --> 00:25:06.253
And yes, it's a head porter
or doorkeeper.

404
00:25:06.288 --> 00:25:09.724
<i>C-O-N-C...</i>

405
00:25:09.758 --> 00:25:12.784
I-E-R-G-E.

406
00:25:12.828 --> 00:25:14.762
<i>That's correct.</i>

407
00:25:18.367 --> 00:25:20.028
You're doing great!

408
00:25:20.068 --> 00:25:22.059
I'm getting lucky.

409
00:25:22.104 --> 00:25:25.232
- I could be getting words like...
<i>- Polydactyly.</i>

410
00:25:29.978 --> 00:25:32.572
We're down to 11 spellers.

411
00:25:32.614 --> 00:25:37.074
The top 10 qualify for
the Southern California regional finals.

412
00:25:37.119 --> 00:25:38.746
In the next round...

413
00:25:42.591 --> 00:25:45.719
- if you miss a word
do not leave the stage...

414
00:25:49.331 --> 00:25:53.768
- as you may have an opportunity
to compete for any remaining places.

415
00:25:53.802 --> 00:25:55.895
Kiana, get that baby out of here.

416
00:25:59.708 --> 00:26:01.733
So...

417
00:26:03.912 --> 00:26:05.903
let's begin.

418
00:26:05.948 --> 00:26:10.749
...L-L-O-G-I-Z-E, syllogize.

419
00:26:10.786 --> 00:26:13.220
That's correct.

420
00:26:23.799 --> 00:26:25.994
"Synecdoche."

421
00:26:29.338 --> 00:26:31.397
Si-neck-do-kee?

422
00:26:31.440 --> 00:26:33.499
Synecdoche.

423
00:26:35.277 --> 00:26:38.110
You wanna tell me
what that means?

424
00:26:38.146 --> 00:26:42.310
<i>A figure of speech</i>
<i>in which a part is used for a whole;</i>

425
00:26:42.351 --> 00:26:45.047
<i>an individual for a class;</i>

426
00:26:45.087 --> 00:26:48.989
<i>a material for a thing;</i>
<i>or the reverse of any of these.</i>

427
00:26:57.432 --> 00:27:00.026
<i>We need you to spell the word.</i>

428
00:27:01.403 --> 00:27:03.701
S...

429
00:27:05.607 --> 00:27:07.871
<i>I...</i>

430
00:27:09.745 --> 00:27:11.713
N...

431
00:27:11.747 --> 00:27:14.181
<i>E...</i>

432
00:27:15.183 --> 00:27:17.515
C...

433
00:27:17.552 --> 00:27:20.316
<i>D-O...</i>

434
00:27:25.227 --> 00:27:27.354
K-E-Y?

435
00:27:27.396 --> 00:27:29.660
Synecdoche?

436
00:27:33.869 --> 00:27:35.564
<i>The correct spelling is</i>

437
00:27:35.604 --> 00:27:38.334
<i>S-Y-N-E-C...</i>

438
00:27:38.373 --> 00:27:41.570
<i>D-O-C-H-E.</i>

439
00:27:48.383 --> 00:27:51.352
<i>If you spell this correctly,</i>

440
00:27:51.386 --> 00:27:53.877
you'll be our 10th and last finalist.

441
00:27:53.922 --> 00:27:56.322
<i>"Carmagnole."</i>

442
00:28:00.395 --> 00:28:02.693
Could I get a definition, please?

443
00:28:02.731 --> 00:28:05.199
<i>A lively song and street dance.</i>

444
00:28:06.435 --> 00:28:07.902
Um...

445
00:28:07.936 --> 00:28:11.064
C... A...

446
00:28:11.106 --> 00:28:13.438
<i>R...</i>

447
00:28:13.475 --> 00:28:16.876
M... A...

448
00:28:27.956 --> 00:28:32.757
G-N-O-L-E,
carmagnole.

449
00:28:32.794 --> 00:28:37.026
That's correct and that means
you are our 10th and last finalist

450
00:28:37.065 --> 00:28:39.363
<i>for the district bee.</i>

451
00:28:42.804 --> 00:28:45.034
They cheated!
They cheated!

452
00:28:45.073 --> 00:28:48.304
I saw them!
That boy's mama helped him out.

453
00:28:48.343 --> 00:28:50.777
<i>What... excuse me.</i>

454
00:28:51.913 --> 00:28:53.938
<i>- Who are you?</i>
- I'm Keelie's sister.

455
00:28:53.982 --> 00:28:57.611
I was standing back there and I saw
that boy's mama give him the letter G.

456
00:28:57.652 --> 00:29:00.120
She was saying "geeeee."

457
00:29:00.155 --> 00:29:03.147
He knew the word!
I mean... it's one we studied.

458
00:29:03.191 --> 00:29:04.715
<i>- He knew it.</i>
- Ma'am...

459
00:29:04.760 --> 00:29:07.888
did you help your child
spell the word?

460
00:29:09.331 --> 00:29:11.799
Ma'am, this is serious business.

461
00:29:15.637 --> 00:29:19.835
Oh, you're damn right it's serious!
You are gonna give these kids ulcers!

462
00:29:19.875 --> 00:29:22.469
<i>Do you know how long</i>
<i>he has studied for this?</i>

463
00:29:22.511 --> 00:29:24.945
- He knew that word.
<i>- No,</i>

464
00:29:24.980 --> 00:29:26.675
I didn't.

465
00:29:30.318 --> 00:29:33.446
I'm sorry, number 62,

466
00:29:33.488 --> 00:29:36.013
you are disqualified
from competition.

467
00:29:36.057 --> 00:29:38.753
Which means, number 54,

468
00:29:38.794 --> 00:29:41.820
if you can spell
one more word correctly

469
00:29:41.863 --> 00:29:45.230
<i>you'll be our 10th</i>
<i>and last finalist.</i>

470
00:29:54.810 --> 00:29:55.936
"Pastiche."

471
00:30:00.549 --> 00:30:05.543
P-A-S-T-I-C-H-E,

472
00:30:05.587 --> 00:30:07.452
pastiche.

473
00:30:07.489 --> 00:30:10.652
That's correct.
That means you've qualified

474
00:30:10.692 --> 00:30:13.422
<i>for the Southern California</i>
<i>regional finals.</i>

475
00:30:17.766 --> 00:30:20.792
Here's my number. We have
a spelling club in my school, maybe you

476
00:30:20.836 --> 00:30:23.566
- should come practice with us sometime.
- That sounds fun.

477
00:30:23.605 --> 00:30:26.574
Yeah, it's cool. Hey, there's my folks.
I'll catch you later.

478
00:30:26.608 --> 00:30:29.406
- Okay. Bye.
- Bye.

479
00:30:29.444 --> 00:30:33.540
Hey, check it out... look at this.
They're gonna be at USC this summer.

480
00:30:33.582 --> 00:30:37.348
Just think, Akeelah, all you've got
to do is place in the top three

481
00:30:37.385 --> 00:30:41.185
of the state regionals,
and you're going to Washington DC.

482
00:30:41.223 --> 00:30:44.056
Not if I can't spell synecdoche.

483
00:30:44.092 --> 00:30:46.356
Uh-oh, y'all, look.

484
00:30:46.394 --> 00:30:49.124
I told you to wear a shirt and tie.
As long as you live in my house

485
00:30:49.164 --> 00:30:50.927
<i>- you do what I say. What did I say?</i>
<i>- Ma...</i>

486
00:30:50.966 --> 00:30:53.059
- No drugs, no gangbangin'...
- Why you stressing me?

487
00:30:53.101 --> 00:30:54.762
- and no $300 watches.
Take it off.

488
00:30:54.803 --> 00:30:56.498
<i>- Derrick-T gave me this. Yes.</i>
<i>- He did?</i>

489
00:30:56.538 --> 00:30:58.335
<i>- Then I'm gonna give it right back.</i>
- Mom?

490
00:30:58.373 --> 00:31:00.102
What?!
You two get inside.

491
00:31:00.141 --> 00:31:02.666
- Take it off.
- But, Mama, I made it.

492
00:31:02.711 --> 00:31:06.044
- Made what?
- Made the cut.

493
00:31:06.081 --> 00:31:08.345
I'm going to the regional bee.

494
00:31:08.383 --> 00:31:12.615
While you're doing that, I'm gonna be
identifying your brother in the morgue.

495
00:31:12.654 --> 00:31:15.088
<i>- No you're not. Ma...</i>
<i>- Yes, I am. Get inside before</i>

496
00:31:15.123 --> 00:31:16.886
I really embarrass your behind.

497
00:31:16.925 --> 00:31:20.622
- Akeelah?
- Akeelah, get in here!

498
00:31:21.997 --> 00:31:23.931
It's okay, Mr. Welch.

499
00:31:25.000 --> 00:31:27.298
I'll see you later.

500
00:31:33.008 --> 00:31:35.101
Guess what.

501
00:31:37.045 --> 00:31:40.014
I'm going to the regional bee.

502
00:33:06.568 --> 00:33:08.695
<i>Dylan?</i>

503
00:33:11.940 --> 00:33:13.805
I'm Akeelah.

504
00:33:17.912 --> 00:33:20.506
Javier said I can come
and join the study group today.

505
00:33:20.548 --> 00:33:23.016
All those rejects do that.

506
00:33:23.051 --> 00:33:25.281
My father coaches me privately.

507
00:33:25.320 --> 00:33:27.413
Who's your coach?

508
00:33:28.656 --> 00:33:31.887
- I don't got one.
- How many spelling bees have you won?

509
00:33:31.926 --> 00:33:34.394
Just the one at my school.

510
00:33:36.765 --> 00:33:39.233
Spell "xanthosis."

511
00:33:40.502 --> 00:33:43.369
- Z-A...
<i>- It starts with an X.</i>

512
00:33:43.405 --> 00:33:46.397
If that idiot hadn't
been caught cheating

513
00:33:46.441 --> 00:33:48.773
<i>you wouldn't have made the cut.</i>

514
00:33:52.647 --> 00:33:56.344
- "Euphoric."
- Euphoric is an adjective.

515
00:33:56.384 --> 00:33:58.375
Origin Greek.

516
00:33:58.420 --> 00:34:00.513
It means like
feeling great and everything.

517
00:34:00.555 --> 00:34:02.887
Either spell the word
or take a shot.

518
00:34:02.924 --> 00:34:06.724
<i>If you miss either one you get a strike.</i>
<i>Three strikes, you're out.</i>

519
00:34:08.029 --> 00:34:11.226
Euphoric, E-U...

520
00:34:11.266 --> 00:34:16.033
P-H-O-R-I-C...

521
00:34:17.105 --> 00:34:19.733
euphoric.
"Psoriasis."

522
00:34:19.774 --> 00:34:24.473
Are you taking Latin at your school?
Psoriasis is a noun, origin Greek.

523
00:34:24.512 --> 00:34:28.346
Are you kidding? My school barely has
enough money for kickballs.

524
00:34:28.383 --> 00:34:32.114
- There's no Latin classes.
- Latin helps you understand words.

525
00:34:32.153 --> 00:34:34.621
Psoriasis is like itchy skin.

526
00:34:34.656 --> 00:34:37.090
Maybe your mom could drive you
up here to take it with us.

527
00:34:37.125 --> 00:34:40.322
I don't know. This is all
starting to sound real heavy.

528
00:34:40.361 --> 00:34:43.421
It is heavy.
Spelling bees are serious shit.

529
00:34:43.465 --> 00:34:45.626
I think I'll take a shot.

530
00:34:53.475 --> 00:34:56.467
On second thought,
could I spell the word?

531
00:34:56.511 --> 00:34:58.638
At Crenshaw they think I'm a freak.

532
00:34:58.680 --> 00:35:01.740
Don't worry.
They think we're freaks here too.

533
00:35:01.783 --> 00:35:03.614
Hey, there's my mom.
Need a ride?

534
00:35:03.651 --> 00:35:07.644
Oh... naw.
My mom's gonna pick me up.

535
00:35:07.689 --> 00:35:09.680
She should be here soon.

536
00:35:11.159 --> 00:35:13.491
Hey, I'm having a birthday party
in a few weeks.

537
00:35:13.528 --> 00:35:15.758
You want to come?

538
00:35:15.797 --> 00:35:18.960
Maybe. If I can.

539
00:35:19.000 --> 00:35:21.195
It was cool seeing you
again, Akeelah. Bye.

540
00:35:21.236 --> 00:35:23.431
Bye.

541
00:35:54.802 --> 00:35:57.532
Where the hell have you been?

542
00:35:57.572 --> 00:36:01.303
- Studying.
- Studying where?

543
00:36:03.144 --> 00:36:05.578
- Woodland Hills.
- Woodland Hills?

544
00:36:05.613 --> 00:36:07.376
What you doing there?

545
00:36:08.483 --> 00:36:10.212
They got a spelling club.

546
00:36:10.251 --> 00:36:12.981
Did Mr. Welch take you?
'Cause ain't nobody call me.

547
00:36:17.525 --> 00:36:20.926
I went by myself. I didn't think
the bus ride would take so long.

548
00:36:20.962 --> 00:36:24.227
- I'm sorry.
- Akeelah Anderson!

549
00:36:24.265 --> 00:36:27.029
You done lost your mind?
You 11 years old.

550
00:36:27.068 --> 00:36:29.832
You don't be taking a bus
to Woodland Hills by yourself.

551
00:36:29.871 --> 00:36:33.568
- There's nobody around to take me.
- That's 'cause I work.

552
00:36:33.608 --> 00:36:35.633
That's what you said
on the weekend.

553
00:36:35.677 --> 00:36:38.544
All the other kids have
their parents at the district bee.

554
00:36:38.580 --> 00:36:42.072
Maybe the other kids have parents
who got more time on their hands.

555
00:36:42.116 --> 00:36:45.677
Look, I'm not having another child
of mine disappearing at all hours.

556
00:36:45.720 --> 00:36:48.951
So if this spelling thing means sneaking
off to the suburbs by yourself,

557
00:36:48.990 --> 00:36:51.254
- I'm calling it all off.
- We can't call it off!

558
00:36:51.292 --> 00:36:54.750
- I'm going to the regional bee.
- Not if you flunk out of school.

559
00:36:54.796 --> 00:36:57.856
I just got a letter saying you gotta
take summer school to make up

560
00:36:57.899 --> 00:37:01.130
- for all these classes you skipped.
- But, Mama, I hate Crenshaw!

561
00:37:01.169 --> 00:37:04.036
It is so boring there
and nobody cares.

562
00:37:04.072 --> 00:37:07.064
But you think they care about you
in Woodland Hills?

563
00:37:07.108 --> 00:37:08.837
At least they got Latin classes

564
00:37:08.876 --> 00:37:10.969
and the kids don't have to study
in their stairwells.

565
00:37:11.012 --> 00:37:14.778
Good for them. But until you finish
summer school at Crenshaw

566
00:37:14.816 --> 00:37:18.081
- ain't gonna be no more spelling bees.
- But, Mama, I got to get ready

567
00:37:18.119 --> 00:37:20.246
for the state bee during the summer.

568
00:37:20.288 --> 00:37:23.052
You just gonna have to do that
next year, now, aren't you?

569
00:37:23.091 --> 00:37:25.889
- But that's not fair!
- Not only is it fair,

570
00:37:25.927 --> 00:37:27.656
it's final.

571
00:37:38.606 --> 00:37:41.769
You'd let me do it.

572
00:38:08.703 --> 00:38:11.194
1979, maculature:

573
00:38:11.239 --> 00:38:14.265
M-A-C-U-L-A-T-U-R-E.
Origin is Latin.

574
00:38:14.309 --> 00:38:18.370
1990, fibranne:
F-I-B-R-A-N-N-E. French.

575
00:38:18.413 --> 00:38:22.577
1996, vivisepulture:
V-I-V-I-S-E-P-U-L-T-U-R-E.

576
00:38:23.618 --> 00:38:25.381
Latin.

577
00:38:25.420 --> 00:38:28.787
I learned all the winning words
since 1925

578
00:38:28.823 --> 00:38:30.791
just like you said I should.

579
00:38:32.026 --> 00:38:34.460
Sorry for being
so insolent last time.

580
00:38:34.495 --> 00:38:36.156
That's not gonna happen no more.

581
00:38:36.197 --> 00:38:38.188
<i>Any... more.</i>

582
00:38:38.232 --> 00:38:40.496
I promise.

583
00:38:40.535 --> 00:38:45.097
I was wondering if you might reconsider
coaching me for the state bee.

584
00:38:47.408 --> 00:38:49.308
'Cause I need a coach.

585
00:38:50.845 --> 00:38:52.676
Bad.

586
00:38:53.748 --> 00:38:56.740
Badly.
You need a coach badly.

587
00:38:57.852 --> 00:39:00.150
Come in.

588
00:39:04.292 --> 00:39:06.192
Come in, come in.

589
00:39:12.867 --> 00:39:16.166
That's a very pretty lady.
Is she your wife?

590
00:39:16.204 --> 00:39:19.696
Listen, you got very lucky
at the district bee.

591
00:39:19.741 --> 00:39:23.006
The competition at state level
is much stronger.

592
00:39:23.044 --> 00:39:26.605
So if you... and I were
to prepare for that,

593
00:39:26.647 --> 00:39:28.842
we'd have to do it on my schedule.

594
00:39:28.883 --> 00:39:32.375
<i>So we'd have to work three hours</i>
<i>each morning starting at 9:00.</i>

595
00:39:32.420 --> 00:39:35.617
<i>- Can you handle that?</i>
- Well, I do have summer school.

596
00:39:35.656 --> 00:39:38.921
But Mr. Welch said working with you
could take the place of it.

597
00:39:38.960 --> 00:39:43.590
Isn't that for students who don't
perform satisfactorily during the year?

598
00:39:43.631 --> 00:39:48.432
Well, sometimes
it's for kids want to get ahead

599
00:39:48.469 --> 00:39:50.903
- for next year.
- Yes, please put that down.

600
00:39:51.906 --> 00:39:53.703
Just... yeah.

601
00:39:57.311 --> 00:40:00.974
Have you...
have you got any goals?

602
00:40:01.015 --> 00:40:02.846
- Hmm?
<i>- Goals...</i>

603
00:40:02.884 --> 00:40:04.818
<i>what would you like to be</i>
<i>when you grow up?</i>

604
00:40:04.852 --> 00:40:07.946
A doctor, a lawyer,
a standup comic.

605
00:40:07.989 --> 00:40:12.426
I don't know. The only thing
I'm good at is spelling.

606
00:40:16.364 --> 00:40:18.355
Go over there

607
00:40:18.399 --> 00:40:21.425
and read the quotation
that's on the wall.

608
00:40:25.039 --> 00:40:26.734
<i>Read it aloud please.</i>

609
00:40:28.409 --> 00:40:32.175
"Our deepest fear is not
that we are inadequate.

610
00:40:33.214 --> 00:40:37.378
Our deepest fear is that we
are powerful beyond measure.

611
00:40:37.418 --> 00:40:39.682
We ask ourselves

612
00:40:39.720 --> 00:40:43.053
'Who am I to be brilliant,
gorgeous,

613
00:40:43.090 --> 00:40:45.650
talented and fabulous? '

614
00:40:45.693 --> 00:40:47.752
Actually,

615
00:40:47.795 --> 00:40:50.491
who are you not to be?

616
00:40:51.532 --> 00:40:53.727
<i>We were born to make manifest</i>

617
00:40:53.768 --> 00:40:56.498
<i>the glory of God that is within us.</i>

618
00:40:56.537 --> 00:40:58.630
And as we let our own light shine,

619
00:40:58.673 --> 00:41:02.439
we unconsciously give other people
permission to do the same."

620
00:41:05.546 --> 00:41:07.673
Does that mean anything to you?

621
00:41:09.116 --> 00:41:12.313
- I don't know.
- It's written in plain English.

622
00:41:13.321 --> 00:41:15.152
What does it mean?

623
00:41:17.091 --> 00:41:20.117
- That I'm not supposed to be afraid.
- Afraid of what?

624
00:41:22.897 --> 00:41:25.229
Afraid of...

625
00:41:27.268 --> 00:41:28.895
me?

626
00:41:37.578 --> 00:41:40.069
This national spelling bee
is a tough nut.

627
00:41:40.114 --> 00:41:42.378
I've seen it chew kids up
and spit them out.

628
00:41:42.416 --> 00:41:45.180
<i>So if you want to get there</i>
<i>you can't be a shrinking violet.</i>

629
00:41:45.219 --> 00:41:47.915
You have to stand up
and show them what you can do.

630
00:41:47.955 --> 00:41:50.116
All right?

631
00:41:52.093 --> 00:41:55.256
And I'll brook no nonsense.
You show up on time

632
00:41:55.296 --> 00:41:58.265
<i>with no attitude or it's over.</i>

633
00:41:58.299 --> 00:42:00.392
Agreed?

634
00:42:02.637 --> 00:42:05.162
Agreed.

635
00:42:12.813 --> 00:42:15.941
Here it is, here it is.
Stop stop stop stop.

636
00:42:15.983 --> 00:42:19.282
Girl, Mom would trip if she knew
we borrowed the car for this.

637
00:42:19.320 --> 00:42:21.686
That's why Mama
ain't gonna know.

638
00:42:23.591 --> 00:42:25.889
<i>Come on, Georgia.</i>

639
00:42:26.928 --> 00:42:29.021
Um...

640
00:42:30.631 --> 00:42:32.861
I'm gonna go
to the mall with Kiana.

641
00:42:32.900 --> 00:42:35.232
But I thought you wanted
to go to this party.

642
00:42:37.672 --> 00:42:39.731
Hey, Akeelah!

643
00:42:43.277 --> 00:42:46.075
- Are you sure?
- Yeah.

644
00:42:47.515 --> 00:42:49.608
Come on, let's go.

645
00:42:49.650 --> 00:42:52.118
How are you?
Oh, thank you.

646
00:43:00.695 --> 00:43:03.755
<i>- Why'd you invite him?</i>
<i>- My dad's friends with his dad.</i>

647
00:43:03.798 --> 00:43:07.393
I'm surprised he showed up.
Hey, you want to see my house?

648
00:43:07.435 --> 00:43:09.630
- Yeah.
- Let's go.

649
00:43:09.670 --> 00:43:12.468
This is my father's office.

650
00:43:12.506 --> 00:43:15.942
He's a journalist and he's written
like a bunch of books.

651
00:43:17.178 --> 00:43:20.170
What's your father do?

652
00:43:20.214 --> 00:43:22.614
My daddy...

653
00:43:22.650 --> 00:43:24.948
he used to work
for the city parks.

654
00:43:26.787 --> 00:43:29.517
Man, you got a lot of friends.

655
00:43:29.557 --> 00:43:31.821
I never had
a birthday party this big.

656
00:43:31.859 --> 00:43:33.759
I'd think you'd have
lots of friends.

657
00:43:39.734 --> 00:43:42.897
- Why'd you do that?
- I had an impulse.

658
00:43:44.171 --> 00:43:46.799
Are you gonna sue me
for sexual harassment?

659
00:43:52.246 --> 00:43:54.942
That was fun.

660
00:43:54.982 --> 00:43:56.711
What are they doing down there?

661
00:43:56.751 --> 00:43:59.618
<i>Oh no, Dylan brought</i>
<i>his Scrabble games.</i>

662
00:43:59.654 --> 00:44:01.315
Scrabble?

663
00:44:01.355 --> 00:44:04.188
<i>I get 30 seconds for each board.</i>

664
00:44:04.225 --> 00:44:07.126
We need one more.
Who wants to play?

665
00:44:07.161 --> 00:44:09.356
I will.

666
00:44:14.902 --> 00:44:17.302
<i>And right out of the gate,</i>
<i>Roman scores 32 points</i>

667
00:44:17.338 --> 00:44:19.704
with "birch" on
the double-word score.

668
00:44:19.740 --> 00:44:23.972
Dylan counters with the immediate
use of Z for 39 big ones.

669
00:44:24.979 --> 00:44:27.470
<i>Polly tests the water</i>
<i>with "acorn."</i>

670
00:44:28.582 --> 00:44:31.915
<i>And Dylan answers with a body blow:</i>
<i>"beacon" for 20.</i>

671
00:44:33.621 --> 00:44:36.021
<i>Holy cannoli.</i>

672
00:44:37.892 --> 00:44:41.191
<i>Right off the bat,</i>
<i>Akeelah uses all her letters</i>

673
00:44:41.228 --> 00:44:43.219
getting 50 extra points

674
00:44:43.264 --> 00:44:46.233
for a whopping 82.

675
00:44:47.368 --> 00:44:49.336
What will Dylan do?

676
00:44:49.370 --> 00:44:51.736
He's fighting the clock.

677
00:44:51.772 --> 00:44:54.263
You could cut
the tension with a butter knife.

678
00:44:54.308 --> 00:44:56.833
Shut up.

679
00:45:04.285 --> 00:45:05.912
Shazam!

680
00:45:05.953 --> 00:45:09.389
Dylan gets
his own bingo for 76 points.

681
00:45:16.997 --> 00:45:20.728
It's come down to this.
After having crushed all five opponents,

682
00:45:20.768 --> 00:45:22.861
Dylan has only Akeelah to beat.

683
00:45:22.903 --> 00:45:26.304
But she's ahead by 21
and has only a few letters left.

684
00:45:26.340 --> 00:45:28.535
Is this an upset in the making?

685
00:45:28.576 --> 00:45:30.771
What kind of birthday party is this?

686
00:45:39.854 --> 00:45:43.790
Yowza! Using the triple word score,
Dylan charges ahead by six.

687
00:45:50.998 --> 00:45:52.966
Just go.

688
00:46:03.144 --> 00:46:06.944
Booyah! Akeelah's in the lead by
12 points and had only one letter left.

689
00:46:06.981 --> 00:46:09.575
<i>But this could be Dylan's final play.</i>

690
00:46:16.023 --> 00:46:18.287
Arrivederci, sweetheart.

691
00:46:21.462 --> 00:46:23.794
12 points ties the game,

692
00:46:23.831 --> 00:46:26.129
but Dylan gets
Akeelah's last two points.

693
00:46:26.167 --> 00:46:29.432
<i>He wins a heartbreaker.</i>

694
00:46:31.672 --> 00:46:34.835
Wow, Akeelah. No one ever
gets that close to beating Dylan.

695
00:46:34.875 --> 00:46:36.843
But I didn't beat him.

696
00:46:38.179 --> 00:46:41.478
Girl, you passed up the mall
to play Scrabble?

697
00:46:44.919 --> 00:46:46.944
<i>She just got lucky.</i>

698
00:46:46.987 --> 00:46:50.923
<i>If you can barely beat a little</i>
<i>black girl at a silly board game,</i>

699
00:46:50.958 --> 00:46:53.358
how do you expect
to win the national bee?

700
00:46:55.429 --> 00:46:57.488
Listen!

701
00:46:57.531 --> 00:47:00.364
We're not coming in second
again this year.

702
00:47:00.401 --> 00:47:03.199
<i>We're going to win.</i>

703
00:47:03.237 --> 00:47:05.603
Let's go.

704
00:47:09.410 --> 00:47:11.207
<i>"He began to have a dim feeling</i>

705
00:47:11.245 --> 00:47:13.475
that to attain his place in the world

706
00:47:13.514 --> 00:47:16.244
he must be himself
and not another."

707
00:47:16.283 --> 00:47:19.480
Dr. Larabee,
this book is too heavy.

708
00:47:19.520 --> 00:47:22.011
Good. It'll develop
your arm muscles.

709
00:47:22.056 --> 00:47:24.183
I thought we were developing
my vocabulary.

710
00:47:24.225 --> 00:47:26.125
We are.
Please continue reading.

711
00:47:26.160 --> 00:47:28.720
But I already know most
of the words in this speech.

712
00:47:28.762 --> 00:47:31.094
It's not a speech.
It's an essay.

713
00:47:31.131 --> 00:47:33.395
By WEB DuBois,
the first black man

714
00:47:33.434 --> 00:47:35.766
to receive a PhD
from Harvard University.

715
00:47:35.803 --> 00:47:37.930
Maybe we should be
studying more big words.

716
00:47:37.972 --> 00:47:43.638
- Spell "cabalistic."
- C-A-B-A-L-I-S-T-I-C,

717
00:47:43.677 --> 00:47:46.202
- cabalistic.
- And when did you learn that one?

718
00:47:46.247 --> 00:47:48.681
About two minutes ago
in this book.

719
00:47:48.716 --> 00:47:51.412
But in the time it took
to learn that one word,

720
00:47:51.452 --> 00:47:53.579
Dylan probably learned 20.

721
00:47:53.621 --> 00:47:56.055
And those 20 words
won't mean anything to him.

722
00:47:56.090 --> 00:48:00.049
He's just a little robot
memorizing lists of words.

723
00:48:00.094 --> 00:48:03.359
The people we are studying:
DuBois, Dr. King, JFK,

724
00:48:03.397 --> 00:48:05.922
these people used words
to change the world.

725
00:48:05.966 --> 00:48:08.662
...the world...
- And they didn't acquire

726
00:48:08.702 --> 00:48:11.535
their vocabulary
by rote memorization.

727
00:48:12.673 --> 00:48:16.575
Okay.
But when I'm at the bee,

728
00:48:16.610 --> 00:48:19.738
and they tell me to spell some
little fish from Australia

729
00:48:19.780 --> 00:48:22.180
or some weird bacteria
on the moon,

730
00:48:22.216 --> 00:48:25.652
we're gonna wish we'd done
a little bit more rote memorizing

731
00:48:25.686 --> 00:48:27.779
and not so much essay reading...

732
00:48:28.822 --> 00:48:31.120
if you don't mind me saying.

733
00:48:33.394 --> 00:48:36.261
Bacteria don't exist on the moon.

734
00:48:36.297 --> 00:48:38.765
<i>Let me ask you something.</i>

735
00:48:38.799 --> 00:48:41.165
Where do you think
big words come from?

736
00:48:41.201 --> 00:48:43.829
People with big brains.

737
00:48:46.240 --> 00:48:49.801
All right, what do you see?

738
00:48:49.843 --> 00:48:51.777
A bunch of big words
I don't know.

739
00:48:51.812 --> 00:48:53.939
Ah, look again.

740
00:48:55.883 --> 00:48:57.908
What kind of power
do we get from the sun?

741
00:48:57.952 --> 00:49:01.251
- Solar.
- So what does "sol" mean?

742
00:49:01.288 --> 00:49:04.485
- Sun.
- What does "terraneous" sound like?

743
00:49:04.525 --> 00:49:06.584
- Terrain.
- Meaning the earth.

744
00:49:06.627 --> 00:49:10.529
"Soliterraneous" means
the sun and the earth

745
00:49:10.564 --> 00:49:13.931
working together.
So where do big words come from?

746
00:49:13.968 --> 00:49:16.903
- Little words.
- And how many little words do you know?

747
00:49:16.937 --> 00:49:18.962
- Tons.
- Yes!

748
00:49:19.006 --> 00:49:21.133
And there are tons more
for you to learn.

749
00:49:21.175 --> 00:49:23.006
There are Greek ones.

750
00:49:23.043 --> 00:49:25.841
There are Latin ones.

751
00:49:25.879 --> 00:49:30.612
French ones.
And if you learn them all,

752
00:49:30.651 --> 00:49:33.313
you can spell any word,
no matter how big.

753
00:49:36.690 --> 00:49:39.181
Maybe we should get back
to the essay reading.

754
00:49:39.226 --> 00:49:42.218
What's the matter?
I thought you wanted to...

755
00:49:42.262 --> 00:49:44.856
win the national spelling bee.

756
00:49:44.898 --> 00:49:48.026
Maybe just getting there
this time is good enough.

757
00:49:48.068 --> 00:49:50.969
Don't give me that.

758
00:49:51.005 --> 00:49:53.565
You want to win it so badly

759
00:49:53.607 --> 00:49:55.973
it keeps you up at night.

760
00:49:56.010 --> 00:49:59.446
Ever since you found out there was such
a thing as the national spelling bee

761
00:49:59.480 --> 00:50:01.812
you've seen yourself
holding up that trophy,

762
00:50:01.849 --> 00:50:04.215
but if you can't say it,
you can't win it. So say it.

763
00:50:04.251 --> 00:50:07.015
- I want to win.
- Say it louder please.

764
00:50:07.054 --> 00:50:09.045
- I want to win.
- You want to win what?

765
00:50:09.089 --> 00:50:11.887
I want to win
the national spelling bee!

766
00:50:11.925 --> 00:50:15.361
Good. Good.

767
00:50:15.396 --> 00:50:18.024
You'll win using my methods.

768
00:50:18.065 --> 00:50:20.898
By first understanding
the power of language,

769
00:50:20.934 --> 00:50:23.232
then by deconstructing it,

770
00:50:23.270 --> 00:50:26.296
breaking it down to its origins,
to its roots,

771
00:50:26.340 --> 00:50:28.831
you'll consume it.
You will own it.

772
00:50:28.876 --> 00:50:31.367
And then you know
what you'll be?

773
00:50:31.412 --> 00:50:33.676
Mm-hmm.
Tired.

774
00:50:33.714 --> 00:50:35.875
You'll be a champion.

775
00:50:37.885 --> 00:50:39.910
- Are you ready?
- I'm ready.

776
00:50:39.953 --> 00:50:41.784
All right, let's go.

777
00:51:42.883 --> 00:51:44.942
Um, why are you home so early?

778
00:51:44.985 --> 00:51:47.249
Just wasn't feeling well.

779
00:51:48.789 --> 00:51:50.757
What you got there?

780
00:51:51.758 --> 00:51:53.157
Homework.

781
00:51:56.530 --> 00:51:58.862
You know, the regional bee
is coming up soon

782
00:51:58.899 --> 00:52:00.890
and I was wondering
if you might want...

783
00:52:00.934 --> 00:52:02.925
Is that gonna happen
in Beverly Hills too?

784
00:52:02.970 --> 00:52:05.598
- I don't know.
- I told you,

785
00:52:05.639 --> 00:52:08.904
you pass summer school then you can
start worrying about the spelling bee.

786
00:52:08.942 --> 00:52:11.308
- But if you would...
- Akeelah.

787
00:52:11.345 --> 00:52:13.939
Baby, I'm just not
in the mood, all right?

788
00:52:26.827 --> 00:52:28.988
So how come
you don't teach anymore?

789
00:52:29.029 --> 00:52:32.021
I told you I do teach. I...

790
00:52:32.065 --> 00:52:34.533
I administer classes online.

791
00:52:34.568 --> 00:52:37.366
Let's keep going.

792
00:52:37.404 --> 00:52:40.373
- "Effervescent."
- E-F-F...

793
00:52:41.808 --> 00:52:43.901
E-R-V-E...

794
00:52:47.381 --> 00:52:48.609
S-E-N-T,

795
00:52:48.649 --> 00:52:50.742
- effervescent.
- Come on, you know this word.

796
00:52:50.784 --> 00:52:52.649
That dog's distracting me.

797
00:52:52.686 --> 00:52:54.950
You're gonna have
much bigger distractions

798
00:52:54.988 --> 00:52:57.013
when you get
to the national bee in DC.

799
00:52:57.057 --> 00:52:59.252
<i>Now what is that</i>
<i>you're doing with your hand, hmm?</i>

800
00:52:59.293 --> 00:53:01.124
What?

801
00:53:01.161 --> 00:53:03.322
With your hand, your hand?

802
00:53:03.363 --> 00:53:04.921
You... you... you...

803
00:53:04.965 --> 00:53:07.661
- you tap, like that. What is that?
- I don't know.

804
00:53:18.078 --> 00:53:20.137
So why do you got
all these toys for?

805
00:53:21.181 --> 00:53:23.445
They belonged to my niece.

806
00:53:23.483 --> 00:53:26.145
Oh. So you got
any kids of your own?

807
00:53:26.186 --> 00:53:28.586
You ask a lot
of questions, don't you?

808
00:53:28.622 --> 00:53:30.749
I'm naturally inquisitive.

809
00:53:30.791 --> 00:53:33.123
Which is also sometimes confused

810
00:53:33.160 --> 00:53:36.459
with being naturally obnoxious.
Here.

811
00:53:36.496 --> 00:53:38.430
Let me see you jump rope.

812
00:53:38.465 --> 00:53:41.366
- Just jump?
- Yes, just jump.

813
00:53:49.343 --> 00:53:51.573
Is there a point to this?

814
00:53:56.583 --> 00:53:58.483
Yes, there is.
Keep going.

815
00:54:03.957 --> 00:54:06.289
I said keep going.

816
00:54:06.326 --> 00:54:08.226
Concentrate.
Stay focused.

817
00:54:08.262 --> 00:54:11.254
<i>Spell effervescent. Don't think</i>
<i>about anything else. Come on.</i>

818
00:54:11.298 --> 00:54:12.663
Go.

819
00:54:12.699 --> 00:54:15.634
E-F-F-E...

820
00:54:15.669 --> 00:54:18.729
- R-V-E-S...
- Good!

821
00:54:18.772 --> 00:54:21.866
C-E-N-T,

822
00:54:21.908 --> 00:54:24.968
- effervescent.
- You see that? That's your trick.

823
00:54:25.012 --> 00:54:27.913
- That's your mnemonic device.
- Jumping rope?

824
00:54:27.948 --> 00:54:29.973
Keeping time, keeping time.

825
00:54:30.017 --> 00:54:32.485
You see kids at the bee...
they do all kinds of things.

826
00:54:32.519 --> 00:54:35.113
They... they sway back and forth.
They...

827
00:54:35.155 --> 00:54:37.316
turn around,
turn around in circles.

828
00:54:37.357 --> 00:54:39.848
Anything they have to do
to stay focused.

829
00:54:39.893 --> 00:54:42.691
You keep time.
And I bet you

830
00:54:42.729 --> 00:54:45.163
if you learned the words
while you kept time

831
00:54:45.198 --> 00:54:49.134
you would remember them
even better.

832
00:54:50.304 --> 00:54:53.569
...L-U-N-I-Z-E.

833
00:54:53.607 --> 00:54:55.541
"Quamdiu."

834
00:54:55.575 --> 00:55:00.638
- Q-U-A-M-D-I-U.
- Good.

835
00:55:00.681 --> 00:55:02.171
Cognizant.

836
00:55:02.215 --> 00:55:04.080
C-O-G-

837
00:55:04.117 --> 00:55:07.553
N-I-Z-A-N-T.

838
00:55:07.587 --> 00:55:09.145
Correct. "Tzigane."

839
00:55:09.189 --> 00:55:13.922
T-Z-I-G-A-N-E.

840
00:55:13.960 --> 00:55:15.222
Correct.

841
00:55:24.438 --> 00:55:26.963
It's too bad your mother had
to work today, Akeelah.

842
00:55:27.007 --> 00:55:28.770
Did she get to see the new dress?

843
00:55:28.809 --> 00:55:32.245
Uh, yeah. She thought it was cool.
Oh, there goes Javier.

844
00:55:32.279 --> 00:55:34.509
<i>This is a very exciting day for us here</i>

845
00:55:34.548 --> 00:55:38.541
because our top three spellers will be
representing Southern California

846
00:55:38.585 --> 00:55:42.919
at the national spelling bee
in Washington DC.

847
00:55:42.956 --> 00:55:44.321
Yes!

848
00:55:46.326 --> 00:55:48.817
<i>So what do you say?</i>
<i>Let's spell!</i>

849
00:55:48.862 --> 00:55:51.695
Well, Josh, you think
she's got a shot at it?

850
00:55:51.732 --> 00:55:53.723
We'll see.

851
00:55:54.768 --> 00:55:58.829
- "Alfresco."
- A-L-F-R-E-S-C-O,

852
00:55:58.872 --> 00:56:01.397
- alfresco.
<i>- That's correct.</i>

853
00:56:03.610 --> 00:56:06.044
Loquacious.

854
00:56:06.079 --> 00:56:07.910
Espadrille.

855
00:56:07.948 --> 00:56:10.314
Jambalaya.

856
00:56:11.585 --> 00:56:13.416
Could you repeat
the word, please?

857
00:56:13.453 --> 00:56:15.353
Malloseismic.

858
00:56:16.656 --> 00:56:18.556
M...

859
00:56:18.592 --> 00:56:20.890
A-I...

860
00:56:20.927 --> 00:56:23.054
<i>O...</i>

861
00:56:23.096 --> 00:56:25.223
<i>S-E...</i>

862
00:56:25.265 --> 00:56:27.426
I-S-M-I-C.

863
00:56:29.436 --> 00:56:31.597
<i>Malloseismic is...</i>

864
00:56:31.638 --> 00:56:33.538
M-A-L-I...

865
00:56:33.573 --> 00:56:37.634
<i>O-S-E-I-S-M-I-C.</i>

866
00:56:38.678 --> 00:56:40.737
It's all right.
It's all right.

867
00:56:43.683 --> 00:56:47.949
<i>D-O-U-B-L-U-R-E.</i>

868
00:56:47.988 --> 00:56:50.479
<i>That's correct.</i>

869
00:56:55.262 --> 00:56:57.162
"Psalmody."

870
00:56:57.197 --> 00:56:59.495
<i>Definition please.</i>

871
00:56:59.533 --> 00:57:03.230
<i>- Psalmody means the practice...</i>
- Mrs. Anderson.
